WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.456
Graph neural networks need many labeled nodes, and
labeling is expensive.

00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:08.491
This ICLR 2022 paper, Information Gain Propagation,
rethinks graph active learning.

00:00:08.571 --> 00:00:12.527
Instead of asking an oracle for a node's exact class,

00:00:12.607 --> 00:00:16.563
it asks a cheaper binary question: does this node
belong to a given class?

00:00:16.643 --> 00:00:20.599
That soft label,

00:00:20.679 --> 00:00:24.634
plus a criterion picking nodes whose information
gain propagates farthest,

00:00:24.714 --> 00:00:28.670
gives higher accuracy at lower cost.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.761
Graph neural networks rely on large amounts of
labeled data,

00:00:32.841 --> 00:00:34.404
which is costly to obtain.

00:00:34.484 --> 00:00:39.270
Active learning cuts this cost by selecting the
most valuable nodes to label.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:44.199
But every prior method assumes an oracle can always
name a node's exact class.

00:00:44.279 --> 00:00:47.548
When categories are many or the domain is unfamiliar,

00:00:47.628 --> 00:00:52.730
that multi-class question is too demanding, and the
budget is spent inefficiently.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.743
Confirming a guess is far easier than naming the
exact class.

00:00:56.823 --> 00:01:00.456
If an expert only answers a binary yes-or-no question,

00:01:00.536 --> 00:01:04.169
the labeling cost per node drops sharply,
especially with many classes.

00:01:04.249 --> 00:01:07.881
Meanwhile,

00:01:07.961 --> 00:01:11.594
existing selection criteria were built for hard
labels and only measure a single node's uncertainty.

00:01:11.674 --> 00:01:15.307
They ignore that in a graph network,

00:01:15.387 --> 00:01:19.020
labeling one node propagates supervision across its
k-hop neighborhood.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.446
The paper makes three contributions.

00:01:22.526 --> 00:01:25.573
First,

00:01:25.653 --> 00:01:28.699
a new active-learning paradigm for graph networks
that uses relaxed queries and soft labels

00:01:28.779 --> 00:01:31.825
instead of exact-class annotation.

00:01:31.905 --> 00:01:34.951
Second,

00:01:35.031 --> 00:01:38.078
a node-selection criterion that maximizes how
information gain propagates through the graph.

00:01:38.157 --> 00:01:41.204
Third,

00:01:41.284 --> 00:01:44.330
experiments showing it beats state-of-the-art
baselines and generalizes across many graph neural network backbones.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:48.218
IGP runs in a loop.

00:01:48.298 --> 00:01:51.806
A graph network is first trained on the labeled
nodes with their soft labels.

00:01:51.886 --> 00:01:55.394
For each candidate, the oracle is asked only
whether the predicted label is correct,

00:01:55.474 --> 00:01:58.982
and that answer becomes a normalized soft label.

00:01:59.062 --> 00:02:02.570
To choose nodes,

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.158
IGP measures how strongly each node influences its
neighbors through propagation,

00:02:06.238 --> 00:02:09.746
the influence magnitude, and combines it with the
information gain,

00:02:09.826 --> 00:02:13.334
the entropy reduction, of every influenced node.

00:02:13.414 --> 00:02:16.922
It then selects the budget-limited subset that
maximizes total propagated information gain,

00:02:17.002 --> 00:02:20.510
updates the model, and repeats.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:26.223
IGP is evaluated on five graph benchmarks: the
citation networks Cora, Citeseer,

00:02:26.303 --> 00:02:29.200
and PubMed, the large social network Reddit,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.448
and the Open Graph Benchmark dataset ogbn-arxiv.

00:02:32.528 --> 00:02:37.861
Crucially, the budget is defined as true annotation
cost, not a count of labels,

00:02:37.941 --> 00:02:41.515
since an exact query is far pricier than a binary one.

00:02:41.595 --> 00:02:44.830
Budgets vary from two to twenty labels per class.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:50.689
Across every dataset and budget, IGP delivers the
highest test accuracy.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:56.402
Under twenty labels per class with a GCN, it
reaches 86.4 percent on Cora,

00:02:56.482 --> 00:03:02.579
75.8 on Citeseer, and 83.6 on PubMed, beating the
strongest prior method, GRAIN,

00:03:02.659 --> 00:03:05.899
by 1.6 to 2.2 percent on citation networks,

00:03:05.979 --> 00:03:10.454
with smaller but consistent gains on Reddit and
ogbn-arxiv.

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:16.090
As budget grows, IGP climbs fastest, and the lead
holds across backbones.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.521
An ablation disables one component at a time.

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:24.114
Removing informative selection, the
propagation-aware node choice,

00:03:24.194 --> 00:03:27.801
causes the largest drop, up to 3.2 percent on PubMed,

00:03:27.881 --> 00:03:30.585
making it the most important ingredient.

00:03:30.665 --> 00:03:35.525
Removing informative training with soft labels
costs up to 2.9 percent,

00:03:35.605 --> 00:03:39.978
dropping the influence-magnitude information
quantity up to 2.1,

00:03:40.058 --> 00:03:43.040
and removing the normalized label up to 2.4.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.710
Every piece contributes.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.487
The headline numbers: 86.4 percent accuracy on Cora,

00:03:48.567 --> 00:03:51.295
a 2.2 percent gain over the best baseline,

00:03:51.375 --> 00:03:55.908
state-of-the-art results across five datasets and
four GNN backbones,

00:03:55.988 --> 00:04:02.460
all achieved by replacing the expensive exact-class
question with one cheap binary query per node.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.279
The takeaway is simple.

00:04:04.359 --> 00:04:06.790
Ask a cheap binary yes-or-no question,

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:11.877
and select nodes whose information gain propagates
farthest across the graph.

00:04:11.957 --> 00:04:16.897
IGP makes graph active learning both more accurate
and far cheaper to label,

00:04:16.977 --> 00:04:19.540
and works with any graph neural network.
